    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Glioblastoma (GBM) is the most common malignant primary brain tumor comprising 40-50% of all adult cases. Despite standard treatment of care involving surgical resection followed by radiotherapy with chemotherapy, the median overall survival rate remains dismal at 14-15 months. The major barrier to effective treatment is GBM tumor metastasis, which enables tumors cells to have enhanced migratory and invasive potential. Recent work has demonstrated a link between Casein Kinase 2 and GBM tumorigenesis, indicating that CK2 modulation could be a promising therapeutic target for GBM. However, no one has studied the role of CK2 in GBM tumor metastatic potential, or the molecular mechanisms underlying tumor cell invasion and migration characteristic of the epithelial to mesenchymal transition (EMT). Here, we investigate the role of CK2 in GBM migration and invasion by using the wound-healing assay, trans well assay, and western blot analysis. We show that CK2 inhibition via CX-4945, a CK2 specific kinase inhibitor, decreases cell invasive potential, and this finding is recapitulated in two immortalized GBM cell lines. Additionally, we show that CK2 upregulation increases expression of mesenchymal markers, suggesting a potential role between CK2 expression, GBM invasiveness, and the EMT pathway. Furthermore, the data represent a major step in elucidating the molecular mechanisms of tumor metastasis, and help identify CK2 as a promising therapeutic target to more effectively treat GBM tumor recurrence.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
